# Slicely — SERVER configuration. Copy to `.env` and edit.
#
# Everything in this file belongs to whoever RUNS Slicely. None of it is a
# visitor's credential, and none of it is ever sent to a browser.
#
# The key that pays for chat is NOT here: each user pastes their own Anthropic or
# OpenAI key into Settings, it is encrypted per session with SLICELY_MASTER_KEY
# below, and their own provider account is billed. See README → "Bring your own
# key". The one exception — YOUR own keys, which fund the metered free credit a
# signed-in visitor gets on a hosted server (and are simply yours on the desktop)
# — is at the bottom, with the four ceilings that bound what it can cost you.

# ── mode ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "hosted" (the default, and what `npm run serve` and Docker use) = a shared
#   server anyone can reach: `__Host-` session cookie, LAN printer discovery and
#   LAN-only transports refused (a datacenter's network is not the visitor's),
#   and no filesystem access outside a session's own workspace.
# "desktop" = the Electron app on one person's own machine, which sets this
#   itself. There is no third value, and no separate multi-user switch: the mode
#   IS the switch (src/main/mode.ts).
SLICELY_MODE=hosted

# ── secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Encrypts each session's stored provider keys at rest (AES-256-GCM). REQUIRED
# in hosted mode. 32 bytes, base64: `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# Rotating it makes every stored key unreadable — users simply reconnect.
# Desktop derives its own into the app's userData directory instead.
SLICELY_MASTER_KEY=

# ── where files live ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Uploads, downloads and sliced G-code, one directory per session beneath it.
# On Fly this is the mounted volume (/data); keep it off the ephemeral disk.
SLICELY_WORKDIR=~/Slicely-data

# ── PrusaSlicer ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PRUSASLICER_PATH=/Applications/PrusaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS/PrusaSlicer
# Your exported printer/filament config, for estimates that match the machine:
# PrusaSlicer → File → Export → Export Config…
PRUSASLICER_CONFIG_INI=
# How many slices may run at once. Each one saturates a core; the rest queue,
# and a request that arrives behind a full queue is told "busy" rather than
# being held open until a proxy times it out.
SLICELY_MAX_SLICES=2

# ── serving ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SLICELY_PORT=3000
# Set to 1 ONLY behind a reverse proxy you control. It makes X-Forwarded-For
# trusted, and BOTH the per-IP rate limits and the session-mint cap depend on
# that address being honest — with no proxy in front, any caller could then hand
# itself a fresh bucket per request.
SLICELY_TRUST_PROXY=0
# Where the site footer's and Settings → About source link points. AGPL §13:
# running PrusaSlicer as a network service obliges you to offer the source of
# what is running. See src/server/LICENSING.md.
SLICELY_REPO_URL=

# ── model sourcing (owner's keys, optional) ──────────────────────────────────
# Raise rate limits / unlock search on the model sites. Never sent to a visitor,
# never reachable from the browser client. Search degrades, it does not break,
# when one is missing.
THINGIVERSE_APP_TOKEN=
GITHUB_TOKEN=
MYMINIFACTORY_API_KEY=
SMITHSONIAN_API_KEY=
# Re-enable the scraped sources (Thangs / Yeggi / STLFinder). They are
# bot-blocked in practice and added ~8s to every search, so they are off.
SLICELY_ENABLE_SCRAPERS=

# ── defaults for the agent ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both are only DEFAULTS; the in-app picker overrides and persists per session.
# SLICELY_MODEL takes any id from MODEL_CATALOG (src/main/settings.ts) —
# claude-opus-4-8 | claude-sonnet-5 | claude-sonnet-4-6 | claude-haiku-4-5 |
# gpt-5.6-terra | gpt-5.6-luna | gpt-6-astra. An unknown id falls back to the
# Anthropic default.
SLICELY_MODEL=
SLICELY_EFFORT=
# How many past turns of a conversation are resent with the next one. 12 is the
# default and applies to EVERY turn, free or paid: an unbounded history is what
# makes the twentieth turn of a chat cost ten times the first, and the model has
# the workspace and its own tools for anything older. Below 1 is a typo and falls
# back to the default.
SLICELY_MAX_HISTORY_TURNS=

# ── sign-in (accounts and free credit) ───────────────────────────────────────
# ALL OF THIS IS OPTIONAL, AND LEAVING IT BLANK IS A SUPPORTED PRODUCT: a hosted
# server with no OAuth client is exactly bring-your-own-key, with no sign-in
# button, no accounts directory and nothing of yours ever spent.
#
# Turning it on takes three ingredients, and the server tells you at boot which
# one is missing (look for one "[accounts] accounts are DISABLED" line):
#   1. this origin (SLICELY_PUBLIC_URL), 2. at least one OAuth client below,
#   3. an owner key under "your own provider keys" to fund the grant.
# /api/config's `accountsEnabled` is true only when all three are present.
#
# THIS APP'S OWN ORIGIN, e.g. https://slicely.fly.dev. Every OAuth redirect URI
# is built from it and NEVER from the Host header, so a spoofed Host cannot move
# a sign-in callback to somebody else's server. It must be a bare https origin —
# no path, no query, no trailing anything — except on localhost, where plain http
# is accepted so the flow can be run without a certificate. Unset = no sign-in;
# set to something unusable = the server REFUSES TO BOOT and says why, because
# the alternative is your visitors reading Google's error page instead of ours.
SLICELY_PUBLIC_URL=
#
# The two OAuth clients. BOTH HALVES of a pair or that provider is not offered —
# half a client is no client. Set one pair, the other, or both; the sign-in card
# shows a button per configured provider, always in this order.
#   Google: console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth
#     client ID → Web application. Authorised redirect URI:
#     <SLICELY_PUBLIC_URL>/auth/google/callback   Scopes: openid, email, profile.
#   GitHub: github.com/settings/developers → New OAuth App. Callback URL:
#     <SLICELY_PUBLIC_URL>/auth/github/callback
# Neither secret is ever sent to a browser, and no provider token is kept: the
# callback reads a verified email and a display name, and throws the rest away.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── your own provider keys ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Chat is bring-your-own-key: each visitor pastes their own Anthropic or OpenAI
# key and their own account is billed. The two variables below are YOURS, and
# what they mean depends on how Slicely is running:
#
#   • DESKTOP APP — you are the only user, so your key is simply used.
#   • HOSTED SERVER — your key funds the FREE TIER and nothing else. It is
#     spendable only by someone who has signed in with Google or GitHub, only up
#     to their one-time grant (SLICELY_FREE_CREDIT_CENTS, 50¢), only on the cheap
#     free model, and only while the global daily ceiling
#     (SLICELY_DAILY_SPEND_CAP_CENTS, $5) is unspent. Every call is metered and
#     written to an audit ledger under <workdir>/accounts/usage/.
#
# A hosted server with no OAuth provider configured spends nothing at all: the
# free tier needs somewhere to sign in, so the app is pure bring-your-own-key and
# a key that is in this file for some other tool is never touched.
#
# SLICELY_ALLOW_OPERATOR_KEY IS GONE. It used to let every visitor of a hosted
# server spend these keys with no limit and no accounting; the metered free tier
# above is the safe version of what it did.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ── what the free tier may cost you ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Four ceilings, each with a conservative default. Leave one blank to take it.
#
# ZERO IS A REAL VALUE FOR ALL FOUR, and it is the kill switch: set any of them
# to 0 and that limit becomes "none allowed" rather than falling back to the
# default. SLICELY_DAILY_SPEND_CAP_CENTS=0 turns free credit off for everybody
# without taking the sign-in buttons away, which is what you want if a bill
# surprises you at 3am. A negative or a non-number is a typo, and falls back.
#
#   SLICELY_FREE_CREDIT_CENTS       50   one-time grant per person, in cents
#   SLICELY_DAILY_SPEND_CAP_CENTS   500  spent across EVERYONE per UTC day ($5)
#   SLICELY_FREE_CHATS_PER_DAY      40   turns one account may start per UTC day
#   SLICELY_SIGNUPS_PER_IP_PER_DAY  3    new accounts per hashed address per day
#
# The other two free-tier numbers must be at least 1 — zero would only break
# chat, not bound it — so a 0 there falls back like any other typo:
#
#   SLICELY_FREE_MODEL                   which model free credit runs on; empty
#                                        follows whichever key above is set
#   SLICELY_FREE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS  4000 per-call output ceiling on free credit
SLICELY_FREE_CREDIT_CENTS=
SLICELY_DAILY_SPEND_CAP_CENTS=
SLICELY_FREE_CHATS_PER_DAY=
SLICELY_SIGNUPS_PER_IP_PER_DAY=
SLICELY_FREE_MODEL=
SLICELY_FREE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=
